      "math_academy_id": "e-8142",
      "problem": "What is the range of $f(x) = -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1 & \\le 1 \\\\ f(x) & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,1].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249143/assets/3ce03253fe40abe25d69866d5b52a621168e3dab2a45ca33d951f1a4551b98c9.png)",
